Job submissions per project

run_estimation.sh commands per project, run from segmentation/ on the cluster.
They call sbatch for the project and activate the env (segamega,
PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1) for the jobs. The last column's command (--segment)
submits both steps at once: the segmentation job waits for the pose job to end
(sbatch --dependency=afterany) and then segments whatever pose files exist. To
resubmit only a segmentation job, use --segment-only with the --seg-* options.
run_estimation.sh picks each job's QOS from its time limit: normal up to 24
hours, medium up to 48 hours, long up to 7 days (only SMILE2's pose job, at
100 hours, needs long).

Estimates are from ../time_estimation.md, fitted on the logs of the three
finished projects: pose estimation takes about 1.4 s per video plus 11.8 ms
per frame with 8 CPUs, segmentation about 3.8 s per file plus 2.5 ms per
frame. Frames = video length x FPS. Requested times are about 2x the estimate,
with a minimum of 1 hour. Jobs that run out of time can simply be resubmitted:
finished files are skipped. Estimates are in hours:minutes.


Pose estimation uses 8 CPUs (also used as --workers), segmentation 2 CPUs.
Memory follows the finished jobs' peaks (sacct MaxRSS): 3.1-4.1G for pose
estimation, about 1.1G for segmentation, on clips of up to 700 frames. Both
grow with frames per file, so projects with long files (SMILE2, SMILE, SRT-2,
SMILE_2018, Noah David: 35,000 to 100,000 frames per file) get more: the pose
data of a file is held in memory, and segmentation processes all of a file's
1024-frame chunks in one batch.

