Notes

Files is the number of transcripts in ../output_stats.csv, which with the
camera selection in project_to_pose.sh should match the number of videos
processed, apart from the notes below. Trainslate and Justisigns are no longer
in output_stats.csv, so they are left out. FPS was read from one video per
project.

(1) InterGaze has 12 transcripts in output_stats.csv, each with a Camera_2 and
    a Camera_3 video, so 24 files and twice the transcripts' length (1:46:24).

(2) Stufen-CDs_Texte has 62 videos for 29 transcripts. The time assumes they
    are parts of the same recordings, so the total length stays the same.

(3) SMILE-Modellvideos has 205 recordings for its 206 transcripts: 99 with
    _S1/_S2/_S7 videos, of which only _S1 is used, and 106 with a single video.
    Its times are measured: the first runs processed all 403 videos (before _S2
    and _S7 were excluded) in 0:22:11 for pose estimation and 0:27:27 for
    segmentation.


How reliable the estimates are

- The fits come from clips of up to 695 frames. Both steps should stay linear
  for long videos: pose estimation works frame by frame, and segmentation
  splits each file into 1024-frame chunks (attention only within a chunk), so
  its time and memory grow with the number of chunks.
- FPS comes from a single video per project. If a project mixes frame rates,
  its estimate changes accordingly.
- Memory is a guess. Check the peak memory of finished jobs (MaxRSS on the
  <jobid>.batch line) with
    sacct -u $USER -S <date> --units=G --format=JobID,JobName%40,State,Elapsed,AllocCPUS,TotalCPU,ReqMem,MaxRSS
  and lower --mem if peak use is well below; smaller requests usually get
  scheduled sooner.
